WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.740
Good morning John, so as you may have noticed,
3D movies are all the rage, and when I say

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.540
rage, in some cases I mean actual, physical,
literal RAGE. I mean really, isn't there enough

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:13.769
3D in our lives already? Almost everything
I have ever known has been 3-dimensional.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:18.140
Seeing a movie in 2D, that just seems like
a novelty to me, a little bit of a change

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.140
- it's refreshing!

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.750
Okay, okay. I actually do like 3D movies,
and I like to go see them with my wife, because

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.410
I like to go see all movies with my wife,
because that's just the kind of couple that

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.820
we are. And so a little while ago when I was
like, "Katherine, let's go see Tron on opening

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.150
night it's gonna be so fun!" She was like,
"Eeunnnnnn," and I didn't get it. Cause of

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.830
course it's not gonna be a great movie, but
it's gonna be a good movie, it's gonna be

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:36.830
fun.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.110
And Katherine, for the first time, let it
slip that she doesn't like 3D movies because

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.790
they give her a headache and I was like "whaaaaa--?!"

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:46.200
So I did some research, and it turns out that
10% of Americans - that's 30 million people

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.090
- experience discomfort while watching 3D
movies, whether it's nausea, motion sickness,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.570
or just a headache. And this was a pretty
revolutionary discovery for me. So what did

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.050
I do about it? Well, I dragged Katherine to
go see Tron and she watched it pirate-style

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.480
for half the movie and still got a headache.
But I did not ignore the needs of my better

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.580
half completely. Because I know how 3D movies
work, and I knew that it would be a relatively

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.980
simple thing to create a pair of glasses that
let you watch a 3D movie in 2D.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.550
So later that week, I called a company that
manufactures 3D glasses. "Yo, can you make

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.390
glasses with the same polarization in each
lens?" "Yeah, I mean, we could, but that would

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.800
totally ruin the 3D effect." "By 'ruin the
effect', do you mean that it would be a crisp,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.240
clear, 2-dimensional image?" "Uhmm... yes?"
"Excellent. I will take 5000."

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.320
I'm not kidding, I actually did it. 2D-glasses.
I am so styliiinnn'! It's kind of a big deal

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:34.470
for me - I had this idea, and then I made
it. I made my idea real! Katherine has watched

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:39.810
several movies with those and with the prototypes,
and she says it's wonderful, and I can't imagine

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.229
she's just saying that because she's my wife,
and I have to say thanks to all the people

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:47.040
who helped make this real. Winnie, at the
factory, Sam, for doing the web development,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.440
Karen, for design. Tim did a great comic that's
up at 2d-glasses.com, my dad, as always, for

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.030
doing the accounting and legal stuff, and
my partner at DFTBA records, Alan, and also

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.160
Chris for helping doing the distribution and
actually getting them to the people who buy

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.160
them.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.450
I made a thing! John, I will see you on Wednesday.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.180
Time for the endscreen and frequently asked
questions. Question one: where can I buy them?

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:13.420
2d-glasses.com. Special introductory offer:
$7.99 per pair, if you buy two, you save two

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:18.490
dollars. Will they work with my 3D television?
No, probably not. I'm sorry, but it's a different

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:22.500
technology that televisions use. Will you
come on my nationally-syndicated talk show

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.120
and talk about your glasses? Oh, of course,
yeah, absolutely - though I don't like leaving

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.520
my house, so, is it okay if I Skype it in?
How on earth does that work? Well, there's

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:36.349
a whole page at 2d-glasses.com that explains
it all to you. Do I have to buy them online?

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.950
Yes, you do, because, it-- the internet is
where it's at, man.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:46.690
Are you excited about Portal 2? Yes I am excited
about Portal 2, look, it comes out in like

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:52.650
12 hours! I will be playing it at youtube.com/hankgames,
the channel where I am currently rushing to

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.680
finish Portal 1, uh, because I want to finish
it before I start Portal 2.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.790
Didn't you forget to thank someone in your
thank-yous? Yes, I did, I forgot to thank

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.200
Victor, who was instrumental in making this
whole thing happen. Why did your lighting

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.300
change so much to the endscreen? Because in
between when I filmed these two things I went

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.510
to go see Rio in 3D; it was awesome. Beautiful
in 3D, but Katherine enjoyed it too, because

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:14.730
it was not painful for her. Because of these.

